<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="biostats-notes-4-1-hyp-test-framework" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Framework for Hypothesis Testing</title>
    
    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Identify and write null and alternative hypotheses in words and symbols. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-hyp-test">
        <statement>
            <p>
                In statistics, a <alert>hypothesis</alert> is a claim regarding a parameter. <alert>Hypothesis Tests</alert> are statistical experiments that determine the validity of a hypothesis.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="def-null-and-alt-hyps">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The assertion that there is no difference between two quantities is called a <alert> null hypothesis </alert> and is denoted <m>H_0</m>. Alternatively, the claim that there is a difference between two quantities <alert> alternative hypothesis </alert> and is denoted <m>H_1</m> or <m>H_A</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        When declaring hypotheses, we follow the below conventions. 
        <ul>
            <li><p> The null hypothesis is the status quo. It suggests that there is no difference between the claim and reality. It always contains an <m>=</m>. </p></li>
            <li><p> The alternative hypothesis is always an inequality. When it contains <m>\neq</m> we call it a <alert>two-tailed test </alert>. Otherwise, when it contains <m> \gt, \lt </m> we call it a <alert>one-tailed test</alert>. It suggests that there is a difference between the claim and reality. </p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <example>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                For each of the following, state the null and alternative hypotheses. 
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The mean annual salary of a data scientist is $130,000. 
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>H_0</m>: The annual salary is $130,000. (<m>\mu= 130,000</m>)
                    <m>H_1</m>: The annual salary is NOT $130,000. (<m>\mu \neq 130,000</m>)
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The mean annual salary of a data scientist is over $80,000. 
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>H_0</m>: The annual salary is $80,000. (<m>\mu \geq 80,000</m>)
                    <m>H_1</m>: The annual salary is greater than $80,000. (<m>\mu \lt 80,000</m>)
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </example>

    <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
            <cell>Trial Procedure</cell><cell>Statistics Procedure</cell><cell>Remarks</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>The prosecution accuses the defendent of guilt in a crime.</cell>
            <cell>A claim is made regarding the relationship between two quantities.</cell>
            <cell>This is usually inspired by some kind of empirical evidence/data.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>All defendents are innocent until found guilty.</cell>
            <cell>We have to assume <m>H_0</m> holds true.</cell>
            <cell>Innocence (no change to criminal status) is the default.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>The prosecution compiles and presents evidence of guilt.</cell>
            <cell>A researcher random samples the population and computes relevant statistics. </cell>
            <cell>In both cases, evidence is being compiled to assert the claim that the status quo is wrong.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>The prosecution must convince the jury the defendent is guilty "beyond a reasonable doubt".</cell>
            <cell>The researcher must show that the computed statistics are "statistically significant". </cell>
            <cell>There is no way the jurors could know with true certainty if the defendent is guilty. But, the prosecution may be able to provide evidence that is damning enough to convince the jury beyond a reasonable doubt. Similarly, if the researcher's statistics are extremely unlikely to occur under the null hypothesis, this may be significant-enough evidence to reject the status quo. </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>The defendent is either found guilty or not guilty.</cell>
            <cell><m>H_0</m> is either rejected or not. </cell>
            <cell>Either the evidence was contrary enough to the status quo or it wasn't.</cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>
        It is important to note that, like in a court of law, it is the burden of the prosecutor to provide signifcant evidence to overturn the status quo (innocence). In statistics, this means that it is our burden to construct an argument to provide significant enough evidence to <alert> reject <m>H_0</m></alert>. We will discuss how to build these arguments in the next section. 
    </p>


    <p> Suppose we have made the argument and reached a decision. Based on our choice, since nothing is certain, we may make an error. There are two types of errors we can make. </p> 

    <definition xml:id="def-hyp-test-errors">
        <statement>
            <p>
                When conducting a hypothesis test, a <alert> type 1 error (false positive) </alert> occurs when we reject the null hypothesis when it was true. Conversely, a <alert> type 2 error (false negative) </alert> occurs when we fail to reject the null hypothesis when it was false. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Reconsider the null hypothesis that <md>\mu = 130,000.</md> What are type 1 and 2 errors in this context?
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                The type 1 error (false positive) would be to reject the null hypothesis when it was actually true. That is, we claim the mean salary is NOT likely $130,000 when in actuality it was. 
            </p>

            <p>
                The type 2 error (false negative) would be to fail to reject the null hypothesis when we should have. That is, we claim the mean salary is likely $130,000 when in actuality it was NOT.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    
    <p> The question remains: What makes evidence statistically significant? </p>



</section>
